{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful and influential ruler navigating complex political landscapes in the Levant. His reign was marked by military campaigns diplomatic maneuvering and economic development solidifying Tripoli's position as a vital center of power.\n\n*Early Life and Accession* Raymond was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Montferrat. He inherited the County upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were characterized by consolidating his authority dealing with internal dissent and defending Tripoli's borders against various enemies including Muslim forces and rival Crusader states. He faced challenges from within his own family particularly from his younger brother Stephen. Raymond skillfully managed these conflicts maintaining his control and ensuring the stability of the county.\n\nRaymond III's reign witnessed significant military activity. He engaged in numerous campaigns against the Ayyubid dynasty led by Saladin. These conflicts often involved sieges raids and strategic alliances. He participated in the Third Crusade although his role was less prominent than that of other European monarchs.  He also dealt with threats from other nearby powers such as the Zengids and the Ilarids.  His military prowess and strategic thinking were instrumental in preserving Tripoli's independence for a considerable period. He understood the importance of fortifying Tripoli and its surrounding territories building strong defenses to withstand potential attacks.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* Raymond III was a shrewd diplomat. He cultivated relationships with various rulers including the kings of Europe and the leaders of other Crusader states.  He sought alliances to bolster Tripoli's defenses and secure its interests. He skillfully played rival factions against each other preventing any single power from dominating the region.  He engaged in extensive correspondence with European monarchs seeking support for his campaigns and diplomatic recognition for his rule.  His diplomatic efforts were crucial in maintaining Tripoli's position within the complex political network of the Levant. He understood the importance of maintaining good relations with the papacy and sought papal support for his endeavors.\n\n*Economic and Cultural Development* Raymond III's rule also fostered economic and cultural growth in Tripoli. He promoted trade and commerce making Tripoli a prosperous trading center. The city's strategic location facilitated trade between Europe Asia and Africa. He invested in infrastructure projects improving the city's defenses and enhancing its economic vitality.  Tripoli became a center of learning and culture attracting scholars and artists from different backgrounds.  Raymond's patronage of the arts and sciences contributed to the city's intellectual flourishing. He encouraged the construction of new buildings including churches mosques and public works. His reign represents a period of relative stability and prosperity for the County of Tripoli leaving a lasting legacy on the region's history. He died in 1199 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00023135847004596144}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5987286790041253e-05}, {"token": "1", "